# Single source of truth for pinned tool + dependency versions LOCAL to this
# package. Sourced by the scripts that need them — bump in ONE place, never
# inline. Plain `KEY="value"` so it's both shell-sourceable and greppable.
#
#   source "$(dirname "$0")/versions.env"
#
# Every entry here is a pinned tool/binary specific to pdf_manipulator's native
# + wasm build or its own platform gate. The GENERIC CI pins (fvm, Chrome,
# bore, and the zizmor + actionlint gate versions) moved to whuppi/ci — the
# shared capabilities + reusable pr-checks carry them from whuppi/ci's own
# versions.env, so they no longer live here.
# Facts about the subject being built (the pdf_oxide crate, its base tag, its
# features) live in build.json instead.
#
# Who bumps what:
#   tool/ci/upgrade.sh (daily PR) — the local pins in this file:
#       PANA_VERSION, NEXTEST_VERSION (+ its sha256, recomputed on bump).
#   whuppi/ci self-upgrade — the shared pins, reaching this repo through a
#       whuppi/ci release + the grouped whuppi-ci Dependabot PR.
#   the shared upgrade-check   — the Flutter SDK in .fvmrc + the lockfiles.
#   Dependabot                 — pub deps + GitHub-action SHAs.
#   RUST_VERSION               — hand-bumped only. It is the engine's Minimum
#       Supported Rust Version; it rises when the vendored crates actually need
#       a newer toolchain, never automatically to "latest".

# The engine's Rust toolchain — ONE pinned version used two ways:
#   • CI installs exactly this (`.github/actions/capabilities/rust`), so builds
#     are reproducible instead of tracking a rolling `stable`.
#   • The build hook's version gate (`ensureCargoVersion`) reads it and refuses
#     a too-old cargo with a clear message, instead of cargo's cryptic
#     `edition2024 is required` (issue #183). versions.env ships in the tarball,
#     so the consumer's gate reads the same line.
# This is the engine's TRUE floor, set by the highest of two needs:
#   • 1.93: the counting allocator (vendor/pdf_oxide/src/host/heap.rs) reads a
#     thread-local. Where a thread-local is a pthread key (Android), std before
#     1.93 allocated its value through the global allocator, which re-entered
#     the allocator until the stack overflowed on the first allocation;
#     1.93 allocates it from the system allocator (rust-lang/rust#144465).
#     No compiler error shows this: only the Android integration tests do.
#   • 1.92: a transitive dep (hayro-jpeg2000, in the render stack) requires it,
#     higher than the vendored crates' own declared `rust-version = "1.88"`,
#     which is stale. Re-probe when the render stack moves:
#     `cargo +<older> build` names the highest requirement.
RUST_VERSION="1.93"

# No compiler-wrapper cache (sccache/ccache) is pinned here on purpose. CI
# caches cargo's target directory instead (`.github/actions/capabilities/
# rust`): a wrapper cannot cache a crate that invokes the system linker —
# bin, cdylib, proc-macro — so the engine's fat-LTO cdylib, the expensive
# step, was rebuilt every job at a 100 % wrapper hit rate, while the
# wrapper's one-entry-per-object writes blew the repository's 10 GB cache
# quota and its unpinned binary broke the Windows web rows on an upstream
# release (argv limit, mozilla/sccache#2397). Do not reintroduce one
# expecting it to cache the engine.

# pana — pub.dev's own analyzer, run by the `platforms` make target + CI job to
# gate that the package still resolves to all 6 pub.dev platforms. `dart pub
# global activate` resolves it from pub.dev (own integrity), so no sha is
# tracked. Bumped by tool/ci/upgrade.sh to pub.dev's LATEST: the gate must run
# the same pana pub.dev runs, or it drifts from the verdict it exists to predict.
PANA_VERSION="0.23.19"

# cargo-nextest — the runner `make test-rust` uses for the vendored engines'
# test suites. `cargo test` runs the ~300 test binaries pdf_oxide produces
# ONE AFTER ANOTHER (only tests inside a binary run in parallel, and most
# hold a handful), so the runner sits mostly idle; nextest schedules every
# test as its own process across all cores. Same test set, same pass/fail
# semantics, plus per-test timings. It does not run doctests — the recipe
# runs `cargo test --doc` beside it, so nothing is dropped. Downloaded
# through fetch_verified.sh against the sha256 below (fail-closed); a
# version bump without a matching hash refuses to install.
NEXTEST_VERSION="0.9.151"
NEXTEST_SHA256_LINUX_X64="1d4ce302b820c41f4b89c5fb9bce45f0f0c94882c1cf08fc360caf08be6e7df4"
